  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nAfter another morning of widespread dense fog, focused mainly on far \nsouthwest and west central Kansas, visibility improved by midday \nwith low clouds continuing to scatter out early in the afternoon. \nPrevailing winds were gradually shifting to the south-southeast in \nresponse to falling MSLP in advance of the approaching upper level \ntrough. The forecast is pretty much a minor update to the ongoing \nforecast with the main change being the addition of counties to the \nFlood Watch -- which now extends east to a La Crosse to Dodge City \nto Meade line as confidence continues to increase in a fairly large \narea of 2 to 3 inches rainfall Tuesday through Thursday.\n\nThe first of the rain showers will approach far southwest Kansas \nearly to mid morning from the southwest which will expand northeast \nthrough the afternoon. Both 12Z HREF and REFS high-resolution \nensemble system suggest local QPF in excess of an inch through late \nafternoon Tuesday, although most locations will see around a quarter \nto a half inch through late in the afternoon. This will just be the \nfirst of several rounds making up this entire multi-day rain event.\n\nAs we head into Tuesday Night, the remnants of Hurricane Polo will \nmove across the Rio Grande and into eastern New Mexico, becoming \ninfused with the larger extra-tropical low approaching from Arizona. \nThe Tuesday Night and Wednesday time frame is when the peak of the \nevent is likely to impact our southwest Kansas area. Rainfall rates \nwill likely be fairly high over a large portion of southwest Kansas, \nespecially west of a Dodge City to Meade line toward the Colorado \nline as low level moisture transport vectors will be maximized with \nPW values 1.7 to 1.8\", well above the 99th percentile for late \nSeptember at Dodge City. Rainfall rates Tuesday Night into Wednesday \nwill likely exceed 2\"/hr with the strongest convection, and there \nmay be quite a bit of it training over some of the same locations. \nThis environmental setting strongly favors flash flooding. This risk \nwill expand northeastward during the day Wednesday into Wednesday \nNight and we have collaborated with neighboring NWS offices GLD, \nGID, and ICT to expand the Flood Watch and extending the watch into \nThursday -- as any additional rainfall later Wednesday Night through \nThursday will lead to additional areal flooding (albeit, not flashy \nin nature).\n\nAs the mid level dry intrusion pushes north across southwest Kansas \nduring the day Wednesday, we will have to watch for redevelopment of \nsurface-based convection later in the afternoon/evening near the \nsurface low. Any direct insolation in the afternoon would allow \ntemperatures to warm to around 80F or slightly higher, and with \ndewpoints in the upper 60s to near 70F, SBCAPE will likely rapidly \nincrease, supporting a small region of severe storm risk near and \neast of the surface low, which by late afternoon/early evening will \nlikely be near Ulysses in far southwest KS. The main upper low will \nlikely still be meandering east across eastern New Mexico into the \nTX Panhandle Wednesday Night into Thursday with even more rounds of \nshower and thunderstorm potential. This is the reason the Flood \nWatch was continued through Thursday as any additional rainfall from \nthe final deformation zone would lead to areal flooding concerns. \n\nWhen all is said and done by late Thursday/Thursday Night, \nwidespread 1.5 to 2.5\" will likely have accumulated across the \nregion. There will also likely be scattered locations with amounts \nin the 3.5\" to 4.5\" range if some areas keep getting hit by each \nsuccessive rounds of rain/thunderstorms. The latest 13Z run of the \nNBM continues to show high 48-hr amounts with 3\"+ probabilities of \n30 to 50% within the earlier discussed Flood Watch region.",
